                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$50-120</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Mid-range composite and carbon</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$120-200</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Premium carbon fiber</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$200+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Flagship and pro signature</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="price-tiers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Four Price Tiers, and What Each One Is For
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Under $50: starter paddles and two-paddle sets</h3>

          <p>
            This tier is dominated by sets: two wooden or fiberglass paddles, a few balls and sometimes a carry bag. Wooden paddles are heavy, often 9 to 11 ounces, and are mostly found in school gym kits and very cheap bundles. Fiberglass and basic composite paddles at this price are lighter and play far better than wood, and some are surprisingly decent for casual games. Expect a smooth face, a thin grip, and an edge guard that may start to peel within months of frequent use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">$50 to $120: the mid-range where most players should start</h3>

          <p>
            This is where raw or textured carbon fiber faces become common, along with 13 to 16 mm polypropylene honeycomb cores, comfortable cushioned grips, and better quality control. Many established brands sell paddles here that share core designs with their more expensive models but skip the newest construction methods. For beginners who expect to keep playing and for intermediate players who want a dependable paddle, this tier usually offers the best value.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">$120 to $200: premium carbon fiber</h3>

          <p>
            Paddles in this range typically use higher-grade carbon fiber faces with more pronounced grit or texture for spin, and many are built with thermoformed construction, where the face, core and edge are fused under heat into a single unibody. Some add foam inserts around the perimeter to enlarge the sweet spot and improve stability on off-center hits. Players who compete in local leagues or tournaments are the main audience, and the differences in spin and stability are real, if modest.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Over $200: flagship and signature models</h3>

          <p>
            At the top end you are paying for the newest construction, the brand's most aggressive surface treatment, and frequently the name of a professional player on the throat. Some of these paddles are excellent, but the step up from a good $150 paddle is small, and premium surfaces can lose some of their grit over a season of heavy play. They make the most sense for experienced players who know exactly what they want from a paddle and can feel the difference.
          </p>

